1
00:00:33,665 --> 00:00:37,551
Only 3% of the
water on our planet is fresh

2
00:00:40,437 --> 00:00:44,242
Yet these precious waters are rich with surprise

3
00:00:57,694 --> 00:01:02,855
All life on land
is ultimately dependent upon fresh water

4
00:01:34,511 --> 00:01:37,832
The mysterious
tepuis of Venezuela

5
00:01:39,834 --> 00:01:44,316
Isolated mountain
plateaus rising high above the jungle

6
00:01:54,311 --> 00:01:59,355
This was the inspiration for Arthur Conan Doyle's lost world

7
00:01:59,395 --> 00:02:02,276
an imagined
prehistoric land

8
00:02:09,042 --> 00:02:13,925
Here strange towers of sandstone have been sculptured over the millennia

9
00:02:13,965 --> 00:02:17,047
by battering wind
and torrential rain

10
00:02:30,548 --> 00:02:34,830
Moisture rising
as water vapour from the surface of the sea

11
00:02:34,870 --> 00:02:37,551
is blown
inland by wind

12
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:51,663
On reaching mountains

13
00:02:51,703 --> 00:02:54,227
the moist air
is forced upwards

14
00:02:54,267 --> 00:02:55,709
And as it cools

15
00:02:55,749 --> 00:02:57,832
it condenses into cloud

16
00:02:57,872 --> 00:02:59,674
and finally rain

17
00:02:59,755 --> 00:03:02,758
The source of
all fresh water

18
00:03:13,493 --> 00:03:18,372
There is a tropical downpour here almost every day of the year

19
00:03:30,251 --> 00:03:33,211
Freshwater's
journey starts here

20
00:03:33,251 --> 00:03:35,131
high in
the mountains

21
00:03:52,142 --> 00:03:55,468
Growing from
humble streams to mighty rivers

22
00:03:55,508 --> 00:03:58,793
It will travel
hundreds of miles to the sea

23
00:04:37,581 --> 00:04:39,062
Angel Falls

24
00:04:39,102 --> 00:04:41,825
the highest
waterfall in the world

25
00:04:56,713 --> 00:05:01,753
Its waters drop
unbroken for almost a thousand metres

26
00:05:11,272 --> 00:05:13,392
Such is the
height of these falls

27
00:05:13,432 --> 00:05:16,071
That long before
the water reaches the base

28
00:05:16,111 --> 00:05:17,831
In the
Devil's Canyon

29
00:05:17,871 --> 00:05:21,031
It's blown away
as a fine mist

30
00:05:53,643 --> 00:05:55,285
In their
upper reaches

31
00:05:55,325 --> 00:05:58,727
mountain streams
are full of energy

32
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:04,572
Streams join
to form rivers

33
00:06:04,612 --> 00:06:06,254
building in power

34
00:06:06,294 --> 00:06:08,095
creating rapids

35
00:06:18,439 --> 00:06:20,560
The water
here is cold

36
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:22,041
low in nutrients

37
00:06:22,081 --> 00:06:24,122
but high in oxygen

38
00:06:28,004 --> 00:06:30,326
The few creatures
that live in the torrent

39
00:06:30,366 --> 00:06:33,007
have to hang
on for dear life

40
00:06:35,445 --> 00:06:38,610
Invertebrates
dominate these upper reaches

41
00:06:38,650 --> 00:06:39,812
The hellgrammite

42
00:06:39,852 --> 00:06:42,617
its body
flattened to reduce drag

43
00:06:42,657 --> 00:06:46,743
has bushy gills
to extract oxygen from the current

44
00:06:51,391 --> 00:06:55,557
Blackfly larvae
anchor themselves with a ring of hooks

45
00:06:59,422 --> 00:07:01,388
But if these
become unstuck

46
00:07:01,428 --> 00:07:05,036
they're still
held by a silken safety line

47
00:07:18,647 --> 00:07:22,527
There are advantages to life in the fast stream

48
00:07:22,567 --> 00:07:26,567
Bamboo shrimps
can just sit and sift out passing particles

49
00:07:26,607 --> 00:07:28,807
With their
fanlike forearms

50
00:07:48,688 --> 00:07:49,249
Usually

51
00:07:49,289 --> 00:07:52,572
these mountain
streams only provide enough food

52
00:07:52,612 --> 00:07:55,455
for small
animals to survive

53
00:07:55,736 --> 00:07:57,618
But with the
spring melt

54
00:07:57,658 --> 00:07:59,059
here in Japan

55
00:07:59,179 --> 00:08:02,142
monsters stir
in their dens

56
00:08:10,961 --> 00:08:12,803
Giant salamanders

57
00:08:12,843 --> 00:08:15,325
the world's
largest amphibian

58
00:08:15,366 --> 00:08:17,888
almost two
metres long

59
00:08:21,212 --> 00:08:25,496
They're the only
large predator in these icy waters

60
00:08:29,821 --> 00:08:32,463
They begin
their hunt at night

61
00:08:47,859 --> 00:08:52,297
These salamanders
have an exceptionally slow metabolism

62
00:08:52,457 --> 00:08:53,936
Living up
to 80 years

63
00:08:53,976 --> 00:08:56,176
they grow
into giants

64
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:16,620
The fish they
hunt are scarce

65
00:09:16,740 --> 00:09:19,740
And salamanders
have poor eyesight

66
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:25,780
But sensory nodes
on their head and body

67
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:29,740
Detect the slightest changes in water pressure

68
00:09:41,043 --> 00:09:43,123
Free from
competition

69
00:09:43,163 --> 00:09:46,363
these giants
can dine alone

70
00:10:00,015 --> 00:10:03,657
Pickings are
usually thin for the salamanders

71
00:10:03,897 --> 00:10:06,659
But every year
some of the world's high rivers

72
00:10:06,699 --> 00:10:10,261
Are crowded by
millions of visitors

73
00:10:17,787 --> 00:10:20,229
The salmon
have arrived

74
00:10:28,024 --> 00:10:32,710
This is the world'
s largest freshwater fish migration

75
00:10:36,996 --> 00:10:38,718
Across the
northern hemisphere

76
00:10:38,758 --> 00:10:42,523
salmon returning
from the ocean to their spawning grounds

77
00:10:42,563 --> 00:10:46,087
Battle their way
for hundreds of miles upstream

78
00:10:51,443 --> 00:10:52,164
Up here

79
00:10:52,204 --> 00:10:56,048
there are fewer
predators to eat their eggs and fry

80
00:11:24,480 --> 00:11:26,121
A grizzly bear

81
00:11:29,082 --> 00:11:31,123
From famine to feast

82
00:11:31,403 --> 00:11:33,324
he's spoilt
for choice

83
00:11:40,565 --> 00:11:43,366
This Canadian
bear is very special

84
00:11:43,406 --> 00:11:46,647
He's learnt to
dive for his dinner

85
00:12:01,619 --> 00:12:04,223
But catching
salmon in deep water

86
00:12:04,263 --> 00:12:05,624
is not that easy

87
00:12:05,664 --> 00:12:08,387
And the cubs
have lots to learn

88
00:12:46,327 --> 00:12:49,051
The annual
arrival of spawning salmon

89
00:12:49,091 --> 00:12:52,736
brings huge quantities of food into these high rivers

90
00:12:52,776 --> 00:12:55,700
that normally
struggle to support much life

91
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:11,340
Although
relatively lifeless

92
00:13:11,380 --> 00:13:14,821
the power of these upland rivers to shape the landscape

93
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:18,542
is greater than
any other stage in a river's life

94
00:13:20,862 --> 00:13:22,382
Driven by gravity

95
00:13:22,422 --> 00:13:25,983
they're the most
erosive forces on the planet

96
00:13:30,466 --> 00:13:33,229
For the past
five million years

97
00:13:33,269 --> 00:13:35,471
Arizona's
Colorado River

98
00:13:35,511 --> 00:13:38,274
has eaten away at
the desert sandstone

99
00:13:38,314 --> 00:13:41,276
to create a
gigantic canyon

100
00:13:52,342 --> 00:13:54,503
It's over
a mile deep

101
00:13:54,543 --> 00:13:56,144
and at its widest

102
00:13:56,184 --> 00:13:59,105
it's 17
miles across

103
00:14:12,752 --> 00:14:14,153
The Grand Canyon

104
00:14:35,049 --> 00:14:39,971
This river has
cut the world's longest canyon system

105
00:14:40,011 --> 00:14:44,973
A 1，000 miles
scar clearly visible from space

106
00:15:11,748 --> 00:15:14,551
As rivers leave
the mountains behind

107
00:15:14,591 --> 00:15:16,232
They
gradually warm

108
00:15:16,272 --> 00:15:19,155
And begin to
support more life

109
00:15:28,216 --> 00:15:33,182
Indian rivers are
home to the world's most social otters

110
00:15:34,264 --> 00:15:39,511
Smooth coated
otters form family groups up to 17 strong

111
00:15:50,805 --> 00:15:54,765
Group rubbing not
only refreshes their coats

112
00:15:54,805 --> 00:15:57,685
but strengthens social bonds

113
00:16:07,606 --> 00:16:09,206
When it
comes to fishing

114
00:16:09,246 --> 00:16:12,286
there is real
strength in numbers

115
00:16:25,507 --> 00:16:29,871
Fishing practice
begins when the cubs are four months old

116
00:16:49,428 --> 00:16:54,830
Only the adults have the speed and agility needed to make a catch

117
00:17:33,916 --> 00:17:38,277
Adults share their catches with their squabbling cubs

118
00:17:49,702 --> 00:17:51,945
Most otters
are solitary

119
00:17:51,985 --> 00:17:54,227
But these
rich warm waters

120
00:17:54,267 --> 00:17:56,950
Can support
large family groups

121
00:17:57,631 --> 00:17:59,993
And even
bigger predators

122
00:18:19,762 --> 00:18:22,644
Mugger crocodiles
four metres long

123
00:18:22,684 --> 00:18:25,324
could easily
take a single otter

124
00:18:51,869 --> 00:18:54,351
But confident
in their gangs

125
00:18:54,391 --> 00:18:58,834
the otters will
actively harass these great reptiles

126
00:19:16,602 --> 00:19:19,565
Team play
wins the day

127
00:19:30,536 --> 00:19:32,258
The Mara River

128
00:19:32,298 --> 00:19:35,701
snaking across
the plains of East Africa

129
00:19:37,852 --> 00:19:39,531
As the land
flattens out

130
00:19:39,571 --> 00:19:43,371
rivers slow down
and lose their destructive power

131
00:19:44,211 --> 00:19:47,690
Now they are
carrying heavy loads of sediment

132
00:19:47,730 --> 00:19:50,170
That stains
their waters brown

133
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:07,061
Lines of wildebeest are on the march

134
00:20:12,199 --> 00:20:12,959
Each year

135
00:20:12,999 --> 00:20:17,641
nearly two million animals migrate across the Serengeti plains

136
00:20:17,681 --> 00:20:20,561
In search of
fresh green pastures

137
00:20:21,082 --> 00:20:22,922
For these
thirsty herds

138
00:20:22,962 --> 00:20:26,564
the rivers are
not only a vital source of drinking water

139
00:20:26,604 --> 00:20:29,605
But also
dangerous obstacles

140
00:20:49,439 --> 00:20:55,406
This is one of the largest concentrations of Nile crocodiles in Africa

141
00:20:55,887 --> 00:20:59,531
Giants that grow
over five metres long

142
00:21:16,085 --> 00:21:17,165
From memory

143
00:21:17,205 --> 00:21:19,805
the crocodiles
know the wildebeest are coming

144
00:21:19,845 --> 00:21:22,405
and gather in
anticipation

145
00:22:27,625 --> 00:22:31,988
The crocodile's
jaws snap tight like a steel trap

146
00:22:32,029 --> 00:22:33,350
Once they
have a hold

147
00:22:33,390 --> 00:22:34,871
they never let go

148
00:22:47,940 --> 00:22:52,341
It took over an
hour to drown this fullgrown bull

149
00:23:00,864 --> 00:23:02,864
To surprise
their prey

150
00:23:02,904 --> 00:23:06,785
crocodiles must
strike with lightning speed

151
00:23:32,242 --> 00:23:33,242
Here

152
00:23:33,282 --> 00:23:38,723
only the narrowest line separates life from death

153
00:24:15,496 --> 00:24:18,258
Most rivers
drain into the sea

154
00:24:18,379 --> 00:24:22,623
But some end
their journey in vast lakes

155
00:24:25,666 --> 00:24:26,747
Worldwide

156
00:24:26,787 --> 00:24:30,111
lakes hold 20
times more fresh water

157
00:24:30,151 --> 00:24:31,952
Than all
the rivers

158
00:24:34,430 --> 00:24:39,398
The East African
Rift Valley holds three of the world's largest

159
00:24:39,440 --> 00:24:41,843
Malawi，Tanganyika

160
00:24:41,884 --> 00:24:43,726
and Victoria

161
00:24:45,651 --> 00:24:46,733
Lake Malawi

162
00:24:46,773 --> 00:24:48,697
the smallest
of the three

163
00:24:48,737 --> 00:24:50,900
is still
bigger than Wales

164
00:25:04,254 --> 00:25:10,058
Its tropical waters teem with more fish species than any other lake

165
00:25:10,899 --> 00:25:14,422
There are 850
different cichlids alone

166
00:25:14,462 --> 00:25:18,144
all of which
evolved from just one single ancestor

167
00:25:18,184 --> 00:25:21,346
isolated here
thousands of years ago

168
00:25:36,241 --> 00:25:40,806
These two metre
wide craters are fish made

169
00:25:50,620 --> 00:25:53,624
Fastidiously
maintained by the males

170
00:25:53,664 --> 00:25:56,586
these bowers are
courtship arenas

171
00:26:09,889 --> 00:26:13,290
Cichlids are
caring parents

172
00:26:17,251 --> 00:26:22,093
Brooding young in
the mouth is a very effective way of protecting them

173
00:26:23,894 --> 00:26:26,894
This lake can be
a dangerous place

174
00:26:39,691 --> 00:26:41,051
After dark

175
00:26:41,091 --> 00:26:46,373
predatory dolphin
fish emerge from their daytime lairs among the rocks

176
00:26:51,616 --> 00:26:53,417
Like packs
of sharks

177
00:26:53,457 --> 00:26:56,858
they're on the
prowl for sleeping cichlids

178
00:27:04,287 --> 00:27:05,367
In the darkness

179
00:27:05,407 --> 00:27:11,047
these electric fishhunt by detecting distortions in the electric field

180
00:27:11,087 --> 00:27:13,367
they create
around their bodies

181
00:27:32,367 --> 00:27:34,623
Any cichlid
that ventures out

182
00:27:34,663 --> 00:27:36,672
will be snapped up

183
00:27:58,434 --> 00:28:04,838
The floor of Lake
Malawi drops 700 metres into an abyss

184
00:28:12,404 --> 00:28:14,365
Here in
this dead zone

185
00:28:14,405 --> 00:28:19,048
the larvae of lake fly midges hide out away from predators

186
00:28:22,698 --> 00:28:23,898
In the
rainy season

187
00:28:23,938 --> 00:28:25,778
they balloon up
to the surface

188
00:28:25,818 --> 00:28:28,938
and undergo a
magical transformation

189
00:28:38,858 --> 00:28:39,698
At dawn

190
00:28:39,738 --> 00:28:43,298
the first adult
midges start to break out

191
00:28:47,662 --> 00:28:48,463
Soon

192
00:28:48,503 --> 00:28:51,946
millions upon
millions of newly hatched lake flies

193
00:28:51,986 --> 00:28:53,747
are taking
to the wing

194
00:29:04,637 --> 00:29:08,240
Early explorers
told tales of lakes that smoked

195
00:29:08,280 --> 00:29:09,881
as if on fire

196
00:29:11,811 --> 00:29:14,052
But these
spiralling columns

197
00:29:14,092 --> 00:29:15,814
hundreds of
metres high

198
00:29:15,854 --> 00:29:17,655
are making flies

199
00:29:32,387 --> 00:29:34,069
Once the
flies have mated

200
00:29:34,109 --> 00:29:36,711
they will all
drop to the water surface

201
00:29:36,751 --> 00:29:38,352
release
their eggs

202
00:29:49,762 --> 00:29:52,843
Molawi may look
like an inland sea

203
00:29:53,083 --> 00:29:56,925
but it's dwarfed
by the world's largest lake

204
00:29:58,405 --> 00:30:00,966
Baikal in
eastern Siberia

205
00:30:13,133 --> 00:30:15,733
400 miles long
and over a mile deep

206
00:30:15,773 --> 00:30:19,133
Baikal contains
one fifth of all the fresh water

207
00:30:19,173 --> 00:30:22,172
found in our
planet's lakes and rivers

208
00:30:25,452 --> 00:30:27,292
For five
months of the year

209
00:30:27,332 --> 00:30:31,372
it's sealed by
an ice sheet over a metre thick

210
00:30:47,616 --> 00:30:49,097
貝加爾湖係世界最古老嘅湖，而且

211
00:30:49,137 --> 00:30:49,777
即使環境咁惡劣，生命喺呢度繁盛

212
00:30:49,817 --> 00:30:51,137
80%嘅種類喺地球其他地方都見唔到

213
00:30:51,177 --> 00:30:52,537
包括世界上唯一嘅淡水海象

214
00:31:12,816 --> 00:31:14,056
With this seal

215
00:31:14,096 --> 00:31:16,818
and its marinelike forests of sponges

216
00:31:16,858 --> 00:31:20,460
Baikal seems more
like an ocean than a lake

217
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:42,562
There are
shrimplike crustaceans

218
00:31:42,602 --> 00:31:44,324
giant amphipods

219
00:31:44,364 --> 00:31:46,205
as large as mice

220
00:31:52,572 --> 00:31:56,055
They are the key
scavengers in this lake

221
00:31:56,095 --> 00:31:58,217
The water here
is just too cold

222
00:31:58,257 --> 00:32:01,740
for the bacteria
that normally decompose the dead

223
00:32:16,269 --> 00:32:19,511
Most rivers do
not end in lakes

224
00:32:19,632 --> 00:32:22,273
But continue
their journey to the sea

225
00:32:27,758 --> 00:32:31,320
The planet's
indisputable super river

226
00:32:31,360 --> 00:32:32,881
Is the Amazon

227
00:32:34,963 --> 00:32:41,131
It carries as much water as the next top ten biggest rivers combined

228
00:32:44,457 --> 00:32:46,620
Rising in the
Peruvian Andes

229
00:32:46,660 --> 00:32:50,384
Its main trunk
flows eastwards across Brazil

230
00:32:50,906 --> 00:32:51,828
On its way

231
00:32:51,868 --> 00:32:55,552
the system drains
a third of South America

232
00:32:56,948 --> 00:32:57,827
Eventually

233
00:32:57,867 --> 00:33:00,865
over 4000 miles
from its source

234
00:33:00,905 --> 00:33:03,863
it empties into
the Atlantic Ocean

235
00:33:11,662 --> 00:33:16,262
The Amazon transports a billion tons of sediment a year

236
00:33:16,302 --> 00:33:20,261
Sediment clearly
visible at the mixing of the waters

237
00:33:20,301 --> 00:33:22,141
Where one
massive tributary

238
00:33:22,181 --> 00:33:23,541
the Rio Negro

239
00:33:23,581 --> 00:33:25,781
flows into
the main river

240
00:33:32,259 --> 00:33:34,778
Its waters are
wonderfully rich

241
00:33:34,898 --> 00:33:35,618
To date

242
00:33:35,658 --> 00:33:39,416
over 3000 species
of their fish have been described

243
00:33:39,456 --> 00:33:42,415
More than in the
whole of the Atlantic Ocean

244
00:34:11,300 --> 00:34:14,662
The Amazon is so
large and rich in fish

245
00:34:14,702 --> 00:34:17,904
that it can
support freshwater dolphins

246
00:34:18,024 --> 00:34:20,506
These
botoes are huge

247
00:34:20,546 --> 00:34:22,546
two and a
half metres long

248
00:34:25,563 --> 00:34:27,164
In these
murky waters

249
00:34:27,204 --> 00:34:30,727
they rely on
sonar to navigate and hunt

250
00:34:48,899 --> 00:34:50,220
They work together

251
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:53,623
to drive shoals
of fish into the shallows

252
00:35:57,625 --> 00:35:59,627
Botoes are
highly social

253
00:35:59,667 --> 00:36:01,349
And in the
breeding season

254
00:36:01,389 --> 00:36:04,271
there is stiff
competition for mates

255
00:36:04,752 --> 00:36:08,315
The males hold
court in a unique way

256
00:36:19,913 --> 00:36:22,517
They pick up
rocks in their jaws

257
00:36:22,557 --> 00:36:25,680
and flaunt them
to their attending females

258
00:36:30,568 --> 00:36:34,935
Maybe each male is trying to show how strong and dexterous he is

259
00:36:34,975 --> 00:36:36,417
and that
he therefore

260
00:36:36,457 --> 00:36:40,061
is the best father a female could have for her young

261
00:36:51,690 --> 00:36:55,609
Successful
displays lead to mating

262
00:37:10,644 --> 00:37:13,527
Even for giant
rivers like the Amazon

263
00:37:13,567 --> 00:37:18,691
the journey to the sea is not always smooth or uninterrupted

264
00:37:47,319 --> 00:37:51,844
Iguassu Falls on
the border of Brazil and Argentina

265
00:37:51,884 --> 00:37:54,967
is one of the
widest waterfalls in the world

266
00:37:55,007 --> 00:37:57,809
one and a
half miles across

267
00:38:04,617 --> 00:38:05,818
In flood

268
00:38:05,899 --> 00:38:10,142
13 million litres
of water spill over every second

269
00:38:58,122 --> 00:39:01,647
All the world's
great broad waterfalls

270
00:39:01,687 --> 00:39:03,609
Victoria，Niagara

271
00:39:03,649 --> 00:39:05,410
And here，Iguassu

272
00:39:05,612 --> 00:39:09,456
Are only found in
the lower courses of their rivers

273
00:39:17,827 --> 00:39:19,509
In their
final stages

274
00:39:19,549 --> 00:39:24,314
Rivers broaden and flow wearily across their flat floodplains

275
00:39:25,582 --> 00:39:27,786
Each wet season
here in Brazil

276
00:39:27,826 --> 00:39:30,630
The Paraná River
overflows its banks

277
00:39:30,670 --> 00:39:33,714
And floods an
area the size of England

278
00:39:37,601 --> 00:39:38,883
The Pantanal

279
00:39:38,923 --> 00:39:41,727
the world's
largest wetland

280
00:39:49,139 --> 00:39:51,342
In these
slowflowing waters

281
00:39:51,382 --> 00:39:53,663
aquatic
plants flourish

282
00:39:53,744 --> 00:39:56,226
Like the Victoria
giant water lily

283
00:39:56,266 --> 00:39:58,948
with leaves
two metres across

284
00:40:22,609 --> 00:40:27,251
These underwater
forests are nursery grounds for fish

285
00:40:29,292 --> 00:40:31,734
Over 300
species breed here

286
00:40:31,774 --> 00:40:34,495
including
redbelly piranha

287
00:40:39,298 --> 00:40:43,340
And other predators like the spectacle caiman

288
00:41:10,595 --> 00:41:12,396
Ripening fig trees

289
00:41:12,436 --> 00:41:14,317
overhanging
the water's edge

290
00:41:14,357 --> 00:41:17,918
provide welcome
food for shoals of hungry fish

291
00:41:25,082 --> 00:41:27,483
The commotion
attracts Dorado

292
00:41:27,523 --> 00:41:31,164
known locally as
the river tiger

293
00:41:38,941 --> 00:41:41,504
They patrol the
feeding shoals

294
00:41:41,544 --> 00:41:44,346
looking for a
chance to strike

295
00:42:22,670 --> 00:42:24,630
And waiting
in the wings

296
00:42:24,670 --> 00:42:27,389
ready to pick off
any injured fish

297
00:42:27,629 --> 00:42:29,189
are the piranhas

298
00:42:41,948 --> 00:42:45,110
A feeding frenzy
quickly develops

299
00:43:03,286 --> 00:43:07,409
Piranha can strip
a fish to the bone in minutes

300
00:43:14,335 --> 00:43:20,378
Great numbers of
fish sustain vast flocks of waterbirds

301
00:43:23,619 --> 00:43:30,743
The rosy at spoonbill is just one of the 650 bird species found in the Pantanal

302
00:43:38,302 --> 00:43:41,064
They nest
alongside wood stalks

303
00:43:41,104 --> 00:43:43,786
in colonies
thousands strong

304
00:44:13,009 --> 00:44:15,735
Spectacle
caiman linger below

305
00:44:15,775 --> 00:44:18,820
waiting for a
meal to fall out of the sky

306
00:45:16,727 --> 00:45:18,849
When rivers
finally reach the sea

307
00:45:18,889 --> 00:45:20,210
they slow down

308
00:45:20,250 --> 00:45:21,651
release
their sediment

309
00:45:21,691 --> 00:45:23,452
and build deltas

310
00:45:25,574 --> 00:45:26,655
In Bangladesh

311
00:45:26,695 --> 00:45:29,817
the Ganges and
Brahmaputra rivers join

312
00:45:29,857 --> 00:45:32,058
to form the
world's biggest

313
00:45:34,780 --> 00:45:35,781
Every year

314
00:45:35,821 --> 00:45:39,384
almost 2000
million tons of sediment

315
00:45:39,424 --> 00:45:41,345
eroded from
the Himalayas

316
00:45:41,385 --> 00:45:43,386
is delivered
to the ocean

317
00:45:48,571 --> 00:45:49,972
At the
delta's mouth

318
00:45:50,012 --> 00:45:53,056
the largest
mangrove forest in the world

319
00:45:53,096 --> 00:45:54,737
the Sundarbans

320
00:46:01,506 --> 00:46:05,791
These extraordinary forests spring up throughout the tropics

321
00:46:05,831 --> 00:46:07,553
In these
tidal zones

322
00:46:07,593 --> 00:46:09,435
where rivers
meet the sea

323
00:46:20,042 --> 00:46:23,806
Crabeating macaques are mangrove specialists

324
00:46:27,972 --> 00:46:29,293
In Indonesia

325
00:46:29,333 --> 00:46:34,218
these monkeys have adopted a unique amphibious lifestyle

326
00:46:42,228 --> 00:46:45,030
They fish
out fallen food

327
00:47:09,065 --> 00:47:11,627
The troop also
uses the waters

328
00:47:11,667 --> 00:47:14,629
to cool off
during the heat of the day

329
00:47:19,752 --> 00:47:22,394
But the channels
are also the playground

330
00:47:22,434 --> 00:47:24,955
for restless
young macaques

331
00:47:31,096 --> 00:47:35,339
Some of the young
have even taken to underwater swimming

332
00:47:41,105 --> 00:47:44,148
They can stay
down for more than 30 seconds

333
00:47:44,188 --> 00:47:47,270
And appear to do
this just for fun

334
00:47:59,972 --> 00:48:02,291
Yet these
swimming skills

335
00:48:02,331 --> 00:48:03,971
acquired
during play

336
00:48:04,011 --> 00:48:06,570
will certainly be
useful later in life

337
00:48:06,610 --> 00:48:09,330
in these flooded
mangrove forests

338
00:48:18,309 --> 00:48:19,829
In cooler climes

339
00:48:19,869 --> 00:48:22,110
mud laid
down in estuaries

340
00:48:22,150 --> 00:48:25,351
is colonised by
saltmarsh grasses

341
00:48:25,391 --> 00:48:29,112
and form one of
the most productive habitats on the planet

342
00:49:01,101 --> 00:49:02,663
400，000
greater snow geese

343
00:49:02,703 --> 00:49:07,069
flock to the estuaries along the Atlantic coast of the United States

344
00:49:07,109 --> 00:49:11,313
To rest and refuel on their long migratory journeys

345
00:49:49,055 --> 00:49:52,496
This is the end
of the rivers journey

346
00:49:52,656 --> 00:49:53,496
Collectively

347
00:49:53,536 --> 00:49:57,218
they've worn down
mountains and carried them to the sea

348
00:49:57,298 --> 00:49:58,698
And all
along the way

349
00:49:58,738 --> 00:50:02,060
their fresh water
has brought life and abundance

350
00:50:02,100 --> 00:50:03,620
To planet Earth

